<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cleruchy">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cleruchy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Klerouchia, Kleruchy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A cleruchy was a type of colony established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classical-greece">Classical Greece</a>, whose settlers, the cleruchs or lot-holders, kept their Athenian citizenship while the community remained a political dependency of Athens. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> the lots went to poor citizens, but inscriptions suggest the cleruchs were more often wealthy men who stayed in Athens while slaves worked their overseas estates. The network was lost at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>. Hellenistic kingdoms, above all the Ptolemies, also granted land to soldiers as cleruchies, and by the mid-3rd century BCE they and their descendants made up up to 29% of the adults of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word comes from the Greek klēroukhos, lot-holder. Greek colonies were normally independent of their mother city, but a cleruchy was given local institutions on Athenian models, such as the council on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>. The first cleruchy is thought to have been Salamis, taken from Megara in the 6th century BCE. Others followed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Thracian Chersonese</a> after its recapture from the Persian Empire and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> after its defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and many more under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> and the Second Athenian League, reaching as far as Amisos on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>.</p><p>The lots were a significant source of private wealth: according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> the 3,000 lots on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> yielded 100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> a year. The network was lost at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> and never reached that extent again, although some cleruchies, such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, were re-established in the 4th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Ptolemaic, Seleucid and Attalid kingdoms also used the system. In Egypt Greeks recruited into the army received land grants, klēroi, on which they lived in peacetime, forming a reserve that could be called up at any time. They were often given marginal land that they had to cultivate, which helped reclaim the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a>. The first were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a>'s roughly 6,500 Macedonian soldiers, and after the Battle of Gaza in 312 BCE Ptolemy settled 8,000 prisoners as reservists.</p><p>Plot sizes followed rank. In the 3rd century BCE native Egyptian machimoi received 5 arouras, infantrymen 30 and cavalrymen 100, and a soldier could be called a 100-aroura man. As grants spread to phylakitai, ephodoi and others, plots shrank and lost their tax advantages, while native soldiers gained land, especially after the machimoi proved themselves at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-raphia">Battle of Raphia</a> in 217 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Cleruchs also formed ethnic communities, politeumata, of Cilicians, Boeotians, Cretans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumaeans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a>. Their descendants, the epigonoi, intermarried with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> and adopted Egyptian religious beliefs, so that by the Roman period much of the Greek population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> was made up of Hellenized Egyptians or people of mixed Egyptian-Greek origins. The Roman authorities regarded them as Egyptians, though a group called the 6,475 katoikoi of the Greek men of the Arsinoite nome kept a privileged tax status. The Fayum mummy portraits, blending Egyptian and Greek traditions, come from this society.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cleruchy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
